<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deerfield, Massachusetts</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deerfield is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> valley of western Massachusetts, settled by English colonists in the 1670s. It was a frontier outpost in the wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, and on 29 February 1704 a French and Native raiding party destroyed much of the village, killing 56 people and marching more than 100 captives to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Some of the captives stayed permanently among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deerfield-massachusetts.jpg" alt="Deerfield, Massachusetts" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jsayre64 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deerfield_MA_post_office.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>For several decades in the late 17th and early 18th centuries Deerfield was the north-westernmost outpost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> settlement. It occupies a fertile part of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> valley, now called the Pioneer Valley, and its position near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a> highlands made it vulnerable to attack. When the English arrived, the area was inhabited by the Algonquian-speaking Pocumtuck nation. The Pocumtuck were much reduced by infectious diseases and by war with the more powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The land was granted to the proprietors of Dedham. John Pynchon obtained a quitclaim deed from the Pocumtuck, though the man who signed, named Chaulk, had no authority to deed the land and seems to have had only a rough idea of what he was signing. Lots were drawn on 23 May 1670, and on 7 May 1673 the General Court made Deerfield a separate town. At the Battle of Bloody Brook on 18 September 1675, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-philips-war">King Philip's War</a>, dispossessed Indians destroyed a small force under Captain Thomas Lathrop, and about 60 colonists died.</p><p>In the predawn hours of 29 February 1704, during Queen Anne's War, French and Native forces under Jean-Baptiste Hertel de Rouville attacked the town. They included 47 Canadiens and 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a>, with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a> and a few Pocumtuck. They razed much of the settlement and killed 56 colonists, then took 112 captives on a months-long trek to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, nearly 300 miles north. Many died on the way, and others were killed because they could not keep up.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Both sides ransomed captives, and the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-williams-missionary">John Williams</a>, who wrote a captivity narrative published in 1707, was redeemed. His daughter Eunice, taken at the age of eight, married a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> man at 16 and stayed with the Mohawk for the rest of her life. Most captives returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, though others remained by choice in French and Native communities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a>. The Pocumtuck Valley Memorial Association was founded in 1870 and opened Memorial Hall Museum in 1880. Historic Deerfield, a National Historic Landmark district, has eleven house museums, and heritage tourism is the town's principal industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deerfield, Massachusetts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor Frontenac repulsed Sir William Phips’s attack on Quebec in 1690, and French and Native raiding parties struck English frontier settlements such as Schenectady in 1690 and <b>Deerfield</b> in 1704.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
